# Authentication and Session Strategy

Covers sign-in, session restoration, secure token storage, logout, and protected routes.

## Requirements

- Keep auth logic inside the auth feature and core session services.
- Store session tokens only in secure storage; must not use `shared_preferences`, plain files, logs, screenshots, or analytics.
- Restore session state at startup before protected route checks finish.
- Refresh tokens before expiry when the backend supports refresh, using a refresh lock to prevent concurrent calls.
- On refresh failure, clear the session and redirect to login.
- Logout must clear sensitive local session data.
- UI route protection must be backed by backend authorization.
- Session context must carry effective permissions (RBAC + ABAC + subscription); guards enforce them per [`permissions.mdc`](./permissions.mdc).

## Session states

| State | Meaning |
|---|---|
| unknown | startup restoration running |
| unauthenticated | no valid session |
| authenticated | valid session and profile available |
| expired | refresh failed or session rejected |
| forbidden | authenticated but lacks permission |

## Acceptance

- Authenticated routes require a valid session.
- Restoration must not flash protected content to unauthenticated users.
- Token values must not appear in logs or UI.

